TI  - VGG19-Driven Artificial Intelligence Framework for Automated Liver Disease Detection and Risk-Aware Clinical Decision-Making Toward Improved Health and Well-Being
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Circuit, Power and Computing Technologies, ICCPCT 2026
SP  - 406
EP  - 411
DO  - 10.1109/ICCPCT70290.2026.11654940
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049013453?origin=resultslist
AB  - Among the most important healthcare problems in the world is liver disease, which is one of the foremost causes of mortality and morbidity, and early diagnosis is a primary solution to effective treatment and the avoidance of the further progression of the disease in question, yet the conventional way of diagnosis is long and presupposes clinical expertise. In this case, the VGG19-trained artificial intelligence system is provided to detect liver diseases automatically and make riskaware clinical decisions. This model has been created based on a labelled dataset of healthy and diseased liver cases as well as preprocessing techniques were employed to progress the quality and consistency of data. It used VGG19 model in extracting the deep features and subsequent classification of the deep features using optimized thresholds and evaluated the model performance in terms of recall, precision, F1-score, accuracy, ROC curve and confusion matrix. The results show that the classification performance is high, the accuracy is high and balance between the recall and precision is good. The confusion matrix is used to indicate that there is good classification of both classes with minimum misclassification and ROC curve indicates that the machine is having large discrimination capability with a high true positive and low false positive. Besides, it is observed that the validation and training curves are stable, and there is no acute overfitting, which reinforces the stability of the model. Overall, the proposed VGG19-based system has enormous potential to become a valuable and viable tool of automated liver disease detection that will lead to the possibility of timely diagnosing and improved clinical outcomes. © 2026 IEEE.
