TI  - Deep Learning framework based High-Performance Monkey-pox Skin Lesion Classification
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Modern Electronics Devices and Intelligent Communication Systems, MEDCOM 2025
SP  - 403
EP  - 406
DO  - 10.1109/MEDCOM67532.2025.11405386
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036642255?origin=resultslist
AB  - Monkeypox, a zoonotic Orthopoxvirus that reemerged globally in 2022, poses investigative challenges due to its clinical resemblance with other pox-viral infections. Rapid and accurate detection is needed to contain outbursts and support timely cure. In this study, we evaluate Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Complex CNN, and DenseNet architectures for automated Monkeypox detection. Investigational results show that DenseNet achieves the best diagnostic presentation, with training and validation accuracies exceeding 97%. In spite of higher computational complication, the model shows strong simplification, with precision, recall, and F1-scores of 0.96-0.97 for both Monkeypox and "Others"classes, and minimal misclassifications. Performance stabilized after approximately 10 epochs, suggesting that early stopping can reduce overfitting while maintaining accuracy. These findings, in fine with recent studies leveraging Transfer Learning (TL) and curated lesion datasets, highlight the potential of Deep Learning (DL) to provide reliable Monkeypox diagnosis. The proposed DenseNet-based approach deals a robust, accurate, and efficient tool to support clinical decision-making and make stronger public health surveillance in handling Monkeypox occurrences.  © 2025 IEEE.
