TI  - XAI-BDPH-Net: Explainable Hybrid Deep Learning for Transparent Personalized Healthcare and Clinical Decision Support
PY  - 2026
T2  - 7th International Conference on Computational Vision and Bio Inspired Computing, ICCVBIC 2026 - Proceedings
SP  - 664
EP  - 673
DO  - 10.1109/ICCVBIC71195.2026.11688286
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052595369?origin=resultslist
AB  - To develop a framework called XAI-BDPH-Net, a hybrid explainable AI model combining SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations) for healthcare analytics that gives transparent predictions and decision support in the context of personalized healthcare. XAI-BDPH-Net consists of a Deep Neural Network (DNN) encoder, a Bidirectional LSTM (Bi-LSTM) for modeling temporal EHR, a weighted ensemble fusion layer, and XGBoost gradient boosting. SHAP gives a global and local feature importance explanation whereas LIME gives an instance level interpretable approximation. The framework is tested on 9,500 multimodal patient records (MIMIC-III, UCI Heart Disease, Pima Diabetes, NHANES), and it is split into 80% train and 20% test; 5- fold cross validation is used as well. Baselines: LR, RF, XGBoost standalone, DNN, Bi-LSTM, BDPH-Net (without XAI).XAI-BDPHNet overcomes the critical black-box limitation of deep learning in clinical settings by mathematically grounded explanations at both a global (population-level) and local (patient-level) scale, which allow clinicians to have confidence and take action on the AI recommendations. The combination of SHAP and LIME guarantees that all predictions and its respective research come with clear feature attributions, facilitating data-driven decision-making and regulatory alignment with FDA AI/ML guidelines and EU AI Act requirements. XAI-BDPH-Net achieves Accuracy = 97.14%, Precision = 96.87%, Recall = 97.02%, F1 = 96.94%, AUC-ROC = 0.9841, Specificity = 97.31%, MCC = 0.9341 outperforming all baselines: LR (76.34%), RF (84.12%), XGBoost (87.23%), DNN (89.41%), Bi-LSTM (91.87%), BDPH-Net-NoXAI (93.12%). This framework correctly classifies 1,841 of 1,900 test cases with just 59 misclassifications (3.1% error rate). The significant potential of XAI-BDPH-Net for explainable AI to aid non-communicable disease management contributes to the UN Sustainable Development Goal 3 (Good Health and Well-being), while its integration of AI to assist in early risk stratification of noncommunicable diseases holds promise for reducing premature death rates among the population, thereby addressing the UN Sustainable Development Goal 10 (Reduced Inequalities).  © 2026 IEEE.
