TI  - Early Detection of Chicken Diseases Using EfficientNetB3: A Deep Learning Approach for Poultry Health Monitoring
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 Global Conference on Information Technology and Communication Networks, GITCON 2025
DO  - 10.1109/GITCON65266.2025.11376834
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035603181?origin=resultslist
AB  - Although the poultry sector is a major source of animal protein, illnesses in hens may cause major food security issues and financial losses. Reducing death rates, improving output efficiency, and stopping the spread of illnesses all depend on early disease identification. Manual observation and laboratory tests are two classic approaches of disease identification that take time and call for knowledgeable staff. This paper suggests to automatically detect four major chicken diseases: Coccidiosis, Healthy, New Castle Disease, and Salmonella using EfficientNetB3, a convolutional neural network (CNN). Comprising 8,067 photos of chickens split into training, validation, and testing sets, the dataset comes from the Kaggle platform. Especially for Coccidiosis and Salmonella, the model attained an overall accuracy of 98% with great precision, recall, and F1-scores. Performance measures and the confusion matrix revealed that the model can minimally misclassify chicken diseases and effectively identify them. Moreover, the training and validation loss and accuracy curves showed that the model efficiently reduced overfitting, thereby attaining ideal performance following seven epochs. These findings imply that EfficientNetB3 offers a strong tool for early illness diagnosis and could be a scalable, effective solution for chicken health monitoring, therefore enhancing management techniques in poultry farming. Aiming to lower financial losses and enhance animal welfare in the poultry sector, this work provides the basis for including advanced deep learning models into practical chicken health systems. © 2025 IEEE.
