TI  - Multi-scale Attention Feature Map based USegNet for Lung Nodule Segmentation and DCFL-CNN for Classification Using Computed Tomography Images
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Journal of Intelligent Engineering and Systems
VL  - 18
IS  - 7
SP  - 678
EP  - 696
DO  - 10.22266/ijies2025.0831.43
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105014026608?origin=resultslist
AB  - Lung nodule segmentation and classification are significant tasks in analyzing Computed Tomography (CT) images to detect symptoms of lung disease. Early detection enables timely treatment, significantly reducing mortality rates. However, existing techniques are inadequate, as variations in shape, size, texture, and location of lung nodules make accurate detection challenging, especially with large volumes of data. Hence, this research proposes the Multiscale Attention Feature Map based USegNet (MAFM-USegNet) to segment lung nodules. The proposed approach enhances segmentation accuracy by modeling fine-grained features at multiple scales and emphasizing larger regions. This flexibility improves the detection of small and irregular nodule boundaries. Furthermore, the Dynamic Coefficient Focal Loss based Convolutional Neural Network (DCFL-CNN) is proposed to classify lung nodules. By integrating attention gates into MAFM-USegNet, segmentation precision is improved by highlighting lung nodules more effectively. Additionally, DCFL-CNN dynamically adjusts the loss function to minimize the misclassification of difficult samples, resulting in higher classification accuracy. The benchmark dataset of the Lung Image Database Consortium and Image Database Resource Initiative (LIDC) is utilized for the implementation and evaluation of the MAFM-USegNet approach. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed MAFM-USegNet and DCFL-CNN achieve a Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC) of 96.73% and classification accuracy of 99.59%, respectively, outperforming existing methods such as BornDenNet and Deep CNN (DCNN). © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
