UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035721940?origin=resultslist
AB  - The Gall bladder cancer is a rare and very aggressive tumour which is usually found at its advanced stages. It will lead to a restricted scope of treatment and low mortality rates. The ability to predict the outcome of the patients accurately is needed by the oncologists so that they can be able to make good health treatment plans and enhance the accuracy of the prognoses. Machine learning-based prediction models are highly significant since they are capable of comprehending complex clinical and diagnostic information. The aim of this article has been to develop a total prediction model that would be able to divide the survival rates of gallbladder cancer patients into two categories, including survivors who are still alive and those who have passed away. It involves an in-depth pre-processing, correct selection of features and optimization of the algorithm to make the process more sensitive and specific. The model efficacy was determined using both calibration and performance curves, which depicts a steady discriminative capacity across classes of targets. The prediction method did a wonderful job to identify patients who had a high likelihood of poor outcomes. It is also quite specific such that people have fewer guesses. This suggests the possibility of benefits of personalized medicine. The model was 96.11% and 94% sensitive and specific respectively, 97.83% and 95.24% precision and negative predictive value respectively, and its F1-score was 0.96. It misclassified only 3.89 percent and that indicates that it is very reliable and clinically significant in terms of predicting the outcome of gallbladder cancer. © 2026 IEEE.
