TI  - Cervical Cancer Diagnosis from Pap Smear Images using Deep Learning and Grad-Cam
PY  - 2025
T2  - 16th International Conference on Advances in Computing, Control, and Telecommunication Technologies, ACT 2025
VL  - 2
SP  - 14866
EP  - 14874
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034865115?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cervical cancer is a major global health concern, ranking as one of the leading causes of cancer-related mortality among women. The effectiveness of early detection through Pap smear tests has been well established. However, manual cytological examination is labor-intensive and prone to human error, leading to misclassification of abnormal cells. This research proposes an automated deep learning-based framework that integrates multiple pretrained Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for Pap smear image classification. The models used include DenseNet121, ResNet50V2, XceptionNet, ResNet101, DenseNet169 & InceptionResNetV2, all of which are fine-tuned for cervical cell classification. To improve interpretability, Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) is incorporated, allowing for visual explainability of model predictions. Experimental results show that ensemble learning significantly improves classification accuracy, reaching over 97%, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed system. The findings highlight the potential of AI-assisted diagnostics in improving cervical cancer screening accuracy and efficiency. © Grenze Scientific Society, 2025.
