SP  - 2069
EP  - 2074
DO  - 10.1093/aje/kwae372
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010000576?origin=resultslist
AB  - To study the risk of spontaneous abortion (SAB) or termination, using health care utilization databases, algorithms to estimate the gestational age (GA) are needed. Using Medicaid data, we developed a hierarchical algorithm to classify pregnancy outcomes. We identified the subset of potential SAB and termination cases, and abstracted the GA from linked electronic medical records (the gold standard). We developed 3 approaches: (1) assign a median GA for SAB and termination cases in the United States; (2) draw a random GA from the population distributions; and (3) estimate the GA based on regression models. Algorithm performance was assessed based on the proportion of pregnancies with estimated GA within 1-4 weeks of the gold standard, the mean squared error (MSE), and the R2 statistic. Approaches 1 and 3 had similar performance, though approach 3 using random forest models with variables selected via the Boruta algorithm resulted in better MSE and R2 values. For SAB, 58.0% of pregnancies were correctly classified within 2 weeks of the gold standard (MSE = 8.7; R2 = 0.09). For termination, the proportions were 66.3% (MSE = 11.7; R2 = 0.35). SABs and terminations can be studied in health care utilization data with careful implementation of validated algorithms; however, a higher level of GA misclassification is expected compared with live births. © 2024 The Author(s). Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health. All rights reserved.
