TI  - Cost-Sensitive Learning for HER2-Positive Breast Cancer Diagnosis Using Clinical Data: A Resourceconscious Framework for Low-Resource Settings
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - 2025 IEEE 12th International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Information Systems, ICICIS 2025
SP  - 614
EP  - 621
DO  - 10.1109/ICICIS66182.2025.11313218
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031777930?origin=resultslist
AB  - Breast cancer remains the most prevalent cancer type among women globally, contributing to high mortality and substantial economic and social costs. HER2-positive (HER2+), accounting for 15-20% of cases, represents an aggressive subtype that worsens prognosis without timely treatment. Although HER2-targeted therapies like trastuzumab have improved survival, early diagnosis remains challenging in developing countries due to limited diagnostic infrastructure and high testing costs. This study investigates a cost-sensitive learning (CSL) framework leveraging routinely collected clinical features from the METABRIC dataset to support HER2+ classification in resourceconstrained settings. After data preprocessing and SMOTEbased class balancing, three CSL models-Decision Tree, Support Vector Machine (SVM), and XGBoost-were trained and evaluated. Despite these adaptations, all models struggled to achieve clinically meaningful performance. SVM attained perfect recall (1.00) but extremely low precision (0.12), misclassifying nearly all HER2- cases. XGBoost offered better precision (0.30) but low recall (0.09), while Decision Tree showed modest balance across metrics. These results underscore the difficulty of detecting minority class instances using routine clinical features alone, even with advanced imbalance-handling strategies. CSL, while valuable, cannot fully compensate for limited feature separability. Enhancing diagnostic reliability may require integration of more discriminative yet cost-conscious and interpretable data sources, such as histopathological or molecular inputs. Nonetheless, this study provides empirical insight into the limitations and opportunities of deploying interpretable, resource-aware diagnostic frameworks in constrained healthcare environments.  © 2025 IEEE.
