TI  - Automated Classification of Lung Nodules in CT Images using DenseNet-121 and Convolutional Block Attention Modules
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 9th International Conference on Trends in Electronics and Informatics, ICOEI 2026
SP  - 1789
EP  - 1796
DO  - 10.1109/ICOEI68323.2026.11541306
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042351356?origin=resultslist
AB  - Lung cancer remains one of the major causes of cancer-related mortality globally and early detection is essential for enhancing patient survival rates and treatment outcomes. A significant proportion of lung cancer cases originate from pulmonary nodules, which are small abnormal growths in lung tissue that may be benign or malignant. Therefore, accurate classification of lung nodules plays a vital role in early lung cancer diagnosis and clinical decision-making. Conventional computer-aided diagnosis systems and many existing deep learning approaches primarily rely on spatial feature extraction, which limits their ability to capture fine texture details and edge characteristics of lung nodules, leading to misclassification and reduced generalization performance. To address these issues, this study develops a DenseNet-121-based framework integrated with a Convolutional Block Attention Module (CBAM) for automated lung nodule classification using Computed Tomography (CT) images. DenseNet-121 is employed as the backbone network to learn hierarchical and discriminative features, while CBAM improves feature representation through channel and spatial attention mechanisms that emphasize informative nodule regions and suppress irrelevant background information. The developed model was evaluated on a publicly available lung CT dataset obtained from Kaggle, consisting of both healthy lung images and lung nodule cases. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves an accuracy of 98.5%, with a precision of 98%, F1-score of 98.4% and a recall of 99%. Furthermore, Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis yields an Area Under the Curve (AUC) of 0.97, confirming strong discriminative capability and highlighting the effectiveness of the proposed model for reliable computer-aided lung cancer diagnosis. © 2026 IEEE.
