UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85214382618?origin=resultslist
AB  - Background: Since the law of January 18, 1994, a deep reorganisation of the health care system for detainees has been underway, with the aim of providing them with a level of care equivalent to that of the general population. However, mortality rates are still higher in prisons than in the general population, especially violent deaths rates. It is essential to collect datas on these deaths in order to define preventive measures. Aims: The main aim of this study is to provide an overview of the causes of death among persons in prison in the Marseille region. Materials and methods: We conducted a retrospective descriptive study, collecting all records of deceased persons incarcerated in 4 penitentiary centers in the PACA region and who were autopsied at the forensic medical institute of the Marseille University Hospital between 2015 and 2020. Results: A total of 79 cases were included, 92 % (n = 73) of them were male, with a mean age of 38.8 years. Forty-nine decedents had undergone a forensic examination at the place of the death (62 %), and 75 had undergone an autopsy (95 %). There were 49 deaths from violent causes (66 %), 19 from natural causes (25 %) and 7 from undetermined causes (9 %). Suicide accounted for 85% of violent deaths (n = 41). Hanging was the method used in 88 % of suicides and the leading cause of death in our study (49 %). Conclusion: Our study provided an overview of the causes of death of people in prison in the Marseille region, where the principal cause of death is suicidal, mainly by hanging, reflecting both national and international existing datas. The recommendations concerning the realisation of a medico-legal autopsy for every inmate's deaths are generally respected, but a forensic examination directly where the body has been discovered, is performed far too infrequently. We have reminded the suicide prevention measures implemented in prisons, which, despite their intensification, have not led to a significant reduction in the suicide rate in French prisons © 2024 Elsevier Masson SAS
