TI  - Machine learning algorithms for early prediction of cervical cancer
PY  - 2026
T2  - De Gruyter Proceedings in Mathematics
SP  - 491
EP  - 510
DO  - 10.1515/9783112221617-024
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047202994?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cervical cancer remains a critical global health concern and a leading cause of mortality among women. Due to significant gaps in screening and healthcare monitoring systems, early and accurate disease prediction is essential for timely intervention and prolonged survival. This study aims to enhance diagnostic precision by evaluating and comparing the performance of four distinct machine learning algorithms for the risk stratification of cervical cancer. The study used a dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository, comprising 36 clinical attributes collected from 858 patient specimens. Four classification models were implemented: multinomial naive Bayes (MNB), Bernoulli naive Bayes (BNB), complement naive Bayes (CNB), and a radial basis function neural network (RBF-NN) utilizing K-means clustering for center initialization. To ensure robust evaluation, a 10-fold cross-validation framework was applied. Performance metrics included validation accuracy, precision, recall, F1 scores, and confusion matrices. Quantitative analysis revealed that the MNB model outperformed all other classifiers, achieving the highest test accuracy of 95.87%. This superior performance indicates that the distribution of the clinical features closely aligns with the model's underlying probabilistic assumptions. The BNB model followed closely with an accuracy of 95.03%, though its handling of binary indicators slightly limited it. CNB achieved 94.30% accuracy, while the RBF-NN (K-means + sigmoid) demonstrated the lowest performance at 92.28%, mainly due to instability related to dataset size and center sensitivity. The empirical results establish MNB as a highly effective, statistically robust, and resource-efficient tool for predicting cervical cancer. Its interpretability and high accuracy support its potential deployment in clinical decision support systems and telemedicine platforms, particularly for early detection in low-resource healthcare settings.  © 2026 Walter de Gruyter GmbH, Genthiner Straße 13, 10785 Berlin, Germany.
