TI  - Morlet Empowering Wavelet Decision Neural Network with Lotus Effect Optimization Algorithm based Classification of Skin Cancer
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Electronics, Communication and Aerospace Technology, ICECA 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICECA66444.2025.11383508
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034832426?origin=resultslist
AB  - Skin carcinoma constitutes the most frequently diagnosed malignant neoplasm globally, rendering timely recognition indispensable for reducing morbidity and mortality and for improving treatment efficacy. Conventional diagnostic modalities, involving visual inspection followed by histopathological biopsy, suffer from inherent invasiveness, potentially lengthy turnaround times, and substantial susceptibility to inter-observer variability. This article presents an advanced hierarchical framework combining a Morlet Empowering Wavelet Decision Neural Network (MEWDNN) with a Lotus Effect Optimization Algorithm (LEOA) to enable fully automated and accurate differentiation of cutaneous tumours. Efficacy is achieved by initially exposing dermoscopic imaging to a Morlet wavelet transformation specifically adapted to emphasise integrated spatial and spectral attributes, thus producing feature vectors with superior class-separability over standard extraction pipelines. Hyper-parameter tuning of the resultant decision network occurs through the LEOA, producing accelerated convergence and demonstrable robustness across disparate dermoscopic cohorts. Extensive validation against benchmark public repositories indicates that the combined MEWDNN-LEOA architecture consistently registers statistical superiority over prevailing algorithms, evincing superior diagnostic accuracy, significantly lower misclassification rates, and negligible alterations in performance across repeated cross-validatory partitions. Collectively, these findings furnish robust evidence in support of the architecture as an independent and non-invasive instrument for the automated primary evaluation of malignant cutaneous lesions in clinical settings.. © 2025 IEEE.
