UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85210255424?origin=resultslist
AB  - Liver disease, a significant health disease often arises from factors such as infections, genetics, and lifestyle choices. It is ranked as the eleventh leading cause of death worldwide, accounting for 4% of global deaths. The use of artificial intelligence (AI) can greatly aid in the early detection of this disease, as it has achieved groundbreaking advancements in enhancing the medical diagnostic processes through its ability to analyze complex medical data. This paper introduces a comparative analysis of 16 machine learning models to improve the accuracy of liver disease classification from clinical datasets, a task conventionally performed by medical professionals. Utilizing a range of algorithms, from logistic regression to advanced deep neural networks, the study examines models' accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-scores, providing a comprehensive evaluation of each models' capabilities. The proposed comparative analysis revealed varying degrees of effectiveness in classifying liver disease; the Random Forest Classifier with PCA & Feature Selection, alongside the Artificial Neural Network (ANN), demonstrated perfect performance, achieving perfect scores of 100% across all metrics: accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. This indicates their excellent ability to classify liver disease accurately without any misclassifications. This study highlights the significant impact that the different AI models have on diagnostic accuracy.  © 2024 IEEE.
