TI  - Optimizing VGG-16 for Early Lung Cancer Detection: A Deep Learning Approach with Enhanced Interpretability
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Emerging Technologies and Innovation for Sustainability, EmergIN 2025
SP  - 402
EP  - 407
DO  - 10.1109/EmergIN67762.2025.11450867
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036373461?origin=resultslist
AB  - Lung cancer continues to be a major cause of death from cancer globally, and late presentation remains a cause for low survival. Screening is hence vital, but established methods of diagnosis, including biopsy and human-driven radiological interpretation, are slow, expensive, and vulnerable to human fault. Recent developments in deep learning and artificial intelligence hold promise for accurate and efficient computerized analysis of medical images.Here, a transfer learning technique from the VGG-16 convolutional neural network (CNN) is utilized for the classification of images from CT scans of the lungs as benign or malignant. Preprocessing was done for the dataset via resizing, normalization, and augmentation for enhanced generalizability. Pre-trained VGG-16 was fine-tuned for the target set of data with adjustment of the fully connected layers for binary classification. Training was carried out for optimization via the Adam optimizer along with regularization methods for minimizing overfitting.Model performance was measured via Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score and ROC-AUC with a marked improvement being realized after optimization. Pre- and post-optimization confusion matrices are readily observable to indicate the reduction of misclassifications, particularly false negatives, critical in detecting cancers. Findings indicate the prospect of VGG-16 transfer learning in the classification of medical images and as a supplement in diagnosis decision-making. Limits in data quantity and generalizability are highlighted, with future studies to focus on larger and diversified data and Clinical Verification. © 2025 IEEE.
