TI  - An Enhanced Deep Learning Framework for Multi-class Breast Cancer Abnormality Detection Optimized with Modified Firefly Algorithms
PY  - 2026
T2  - Annals of Data Science
DO  - 10.1007/s40745-026-00724-1
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052850327?origin=resultslist
AB  - Breast cancer is still one of the leading causes of death for women across the globe, and it is therefore very important to develop a reliable and efficient diagnostic system to identify it early and correctly. In this paper, an optimized deep learning system for multi-class abnormality classification for breast cancer is proposed based on an optimized Inception V3 model using a self-attention mechanism, which is further fine-tuned for higher classification accuracy and faster convergence using three different versions of the firefly algorithm (MVFA). The attention mechanism is integrated with Inception V3 by inserting an attention module after two Inception blocks and one immediately before the final classification layer. When both channel and spatial attention are combined, the feature maps are sequentially refined by channel attention followed by spatial attention before being passed to the next Inception block or the classifier. The proposed architecture is fine-tuned using MVFA for reliable, robust and accurate classification of breast cancer in BUSI and CBIS-DDSM public datasets. The proposed framework performed significantly better than the current state-of-the-art reported systems that apply deep CNN, image decomposition techniques, semi-supervised GAN, and DarkNet-53 deep learning systems optimized by SVM and SGM techniques. The proposed results are also compared with state-of-the-art pre-trained models and shows improvement in terms of overall accuracy, F1-score, recall, and precision. Comprehensive Monte Carlo simulations also validate the reliability and robustness of the model by showing consistency in its performance in independent runs. The deep learning model optimized by MVFA-III thus proves to be a highly efficient assistive model for radiologists in breast cancer diagnosis with increased accuracy and efficiency and decreased misclassifications. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026.
