TI  - Recognizing atrial flutter in the emergency department: Challenges in diagnosis
PY  - 2025
T2  - American Journal of Emergency Medicine
VL  - 96
SP  - 224
EP  - 229
DO  - 10.1016/j.ajem.2025.07.013
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105009821283?origin=resultslist
AB  - Introduction: Aside from anticoagulation, atrial fibrillation (AF) and atrial flutter (AFL) require distinct management. Yet, misclassification between the two is not uncommon, potentially resulting in suboptimal care. Computerized interpretation of the ECG (CIE) may mislead clinicians. This study aimed to assess the diagnostic accuracy of AFL in an emergnecy department (ED) environment free of CIE influence and to identify predictors of misdiagnosis. Methods: ECGs from consecutive patients discharged with AF or AFL (December 2019– December 2024) were reviewed. Two cardiologists independently reinterpreted the tracings, with an electrophysiologist as an arbitrator. Results: Of 2037 ECGs reviewed, 31 were excluded due to paced rhythms or indeterminate diagnoses, leaving 2003 for analysis. Among these, 1794 ECGs carried a discharge diagnosis of AF and 209 AFL. Re-evaluation confirmed only 55.6 % of AFL cases were correctly identified, while 141 cases (44.4 %) were erroneously interpreted as AF. Misdiagnosed patients were older (p = 0.039), more likely to have a prior AF diagnosis (p = 0.007), and more frequently exhibited variable atrioventricular (AV) conduction (52.5 % vs. 32.3 %, p < 0.001). Variable AV conduction independently predicted misdiagnosis, with an odds ratio of 2.47 (95 % CI: 1.46–4.17, p < 0.001). Conclusion: Even in the absence of CIE, AFL remains frequently misdiagnosed as AF in the ED. Targeted physician education on irregular ventricular response, a critical independent predictor of this misdiagnosis, is imperative to optimize patient care. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.
