SP  - 1627
EP  - 1632
DO  - 10.1109/ICAUC68182.2026.11440937
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037451611?origin=resultslist
AB  - Early and accurate diagnosis of skin cancer is clinically critical, as delayed or incorrect detection, particularly of aggressive lesions such as melanoma, can significantly increase morbidity and mortality. This work presents a deep learning based comparative analysis of three convolutional neural network architectures, namely MobileNet, EfficientNet, and DenseNet, for multiclass skin lesion classification using the HAM10000 dermatoscopic image dataset comprising seven lesion categories. The models are systematically evaluated under varying training testing splits and multiple training epochs to analyze performance consistency, convergence behaviour, and generalization capability. Experimental results show that MobileNet achieves the highest overall accuracy of 94.9% at a 90:10 split, followed closely by EfficientNet with 94.8% and DenseNet with 94.2%. However, detailed class wise analysis reveals significantly lower precision and recall for clinically critical minority classes such as melanoma and actinic keratosis, despite high overall accuracy. This observation highlights the limitation of relying solely on accuracy for medical decision making. The study further provides confusion matrix based insights into misclassification patterns and discusses their clinical implications. The findings establish a foundation for translating convolutional neural network based skin lesion classifiers into reliable clinical decision support systems while identifying key challenges related to class imbalance and diagnostic risk.  © 2026 IEEE.
