TI  - Multi-Scale Contextual Feature Fusion Network for Robust Skin Lesion Recognition
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 10th International Conference on Science Technology, Engineering and Mathematics, ICONSTEM 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICONSTEM65670.2025.11374545
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034494993?origin=resultslist
AB  - To increase the quality of the diagnostics and reduce the mortality rates, melanoma must be identified at the initial phase of its development, yet the automated skin lesion analysis is becoming a challenging task due to irregular structure, inter-class similarity, and imaging artifacts. In this paper, the proposed multi-scale contextual feature fusion network (MCFF-Net) that has proven to be effective in the recognition of skin lesions is proposed as the solution to these problems. Compared to the more traditional multi-scale methodologies, MCFFNet is a model that contains a contextual attention mechanism that aids in the capture of the semantic dependence between lesion areas and their surroundings to ensure the further differentiation of visually similar classes. It is based on the idea of learning both finegrained textures and deep semantic details, using a hierarchical multi-scale extraction block and then adapting to combine shallow and deep features without lesion edges, an idea referred to as context-guided fusion. A refining process further cancels the background noise and sharpens the contours in order to make it robust in a wide imaging condition. It is highly tested over benchmark dermoscopic datasets, such as ISIC2018 and HAM10000. Experimental findings indicate that MCFFNet has a total accuracy of 98.3%, better sensitivity, specificity and AUC than state-of-the-art techniques. The combination of contextual feature fusion can considerably improve the boundary delineation and misclassification rates and makes MCFF-Net a promising and stable framework to be used in clinical practice to detect early-stage melanoma. © 2025 IEEE.
