TI  - A Meta Learning Based Stacked Ensemble Model (MetaCervX) for Enhanced Cervical Cancer Prediction
PY  - 2025
T2  - ISED 2025 - 13th International Conference on Intelligent Systems and Embedded Design, Proceedings
SP  - 74
EP  - 80
DO  - 10.1109/ISED67359.2025.11404865
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035831739?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cervical cancer remains a significant contributor to global cancer mortality in women, ranking second after breast cancer. A higher survival rate and successful treatment depend on early detection. Machine learning techniques help in detecting cervical cancer in early treatable stages and ultimately lead to better treatment of the disease. This study uses a stacked ensemble learning model with Logistic Regression (LR) as the meta model and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Extra Trees Classifier (ETC), and Random Forest (RF) as base models to accurately diagnose cervical cancer, thereby enhancing early detection and reducing false negatives. This work utilizes a cervical cancer risk factor dataset that contains missing values and class imbalance, which impact the reliability of the model. SMOTE and SMOTE-ENN are utilized by the MetaCervX model to address these issues and stabilize the data, while a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) imputer is used to fill in the missing values, resulting in more reliable and consistent model performance. The accuracy was found to be 95.93%, while the sensitivity was barely 45.55% in the initial results with row-imbalanced data. The model's performance was enhanced by SMOTE by increasing the sensitivity to 98.14% and the accuracy to 98.76%. The final stacked ensemble model (MetaCervX), combined with SMOTEENN, showed an accuracy of 99.29% and ideal sensitivity. The noisy and misclassified data were refined by the ENN component, which reduces class overlap and improves the overall quality of the dataset. This improvement allowed the MetaCervX (SMOTE-ENN) model to achieve stable and reliable learning, showing improved performance over the SMOTE-based version. These major things that these experiments demonstrate are that MetaCervX with SMOTE-ENN effectively increases cervical cancer detection, resulting in improved diagnostic and treatment strategies. © 2025 IEEE.
