TI  - Hybrid Deep Learning and ANFIS-Based Framework for Uncertainty-Aware Breast Cancer Detection in Mammography
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Biennial Conference on Advances in Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, IBAAIDS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/IBAAIDS66771.2026.11567428
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044215766?origin=resultslist
AB  - Breast cancer remains a leading cause of morbidity and mortality, with early detection crucial for improving survival rates. Mammography is the gold standard for screening, yet its diagnostic accuracy depends heavily on radiologist expertise and faces challenges like inter-observer variability and dense breast tissues. Recent advances in deep learning (DL) have enhanced breast cancer detection, but existing systems lack transparency, interpretability, and the ability to quantify prediction uncertainty. These limitations hinder their clinical adoption. This study proposes a novel framework integrating hybrid deep learning with an Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference System (ANFIS) to enable uncertaintyaware and interpretable breast cancer detection. The proposed model combines deep neural networks (CNNs, residual networks, transformers) for feature extraction with ANFIS's rule-based transparency, bridging high-performance classification with interpretability necessary for clinical integration. By incorporating uncertainty quantification techniques such as Bayesian DL, Monte Carlo dropout, and deep ensembles, the framework allows the system to express both prediction likelihood and associated confidence, reducing overconfidence in misclassifications. © 2026 IEEE.
