TI  - CoRIS: A Cost-Aware and Explainable Ordinal Machine Learning Framework for Maternal Health Risk Prediction
PY  - 2026
T2  - IISE Annual Conference and Expo 2026
VL  - 4
SP  - 2003
EP  - 2009
DO  - 10.21872/2026IISE_17341
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052143573?origin=resultslist
AB  - Maternal health complications remain a major global concern, and timely identification of high-risk conditions is essential for reducing mortality and improving outcomes. Although several studies have applied machine learning (ML) techniques to predict maternal risk, most have treated risk categories as independent classes, which ignores their natural ordinal progression and the unequal cost of misclassification. Therefore, this study proposes a cost-aware learning, ordinal, and explainable ML framework for predicting maternal health risk levels based on physiological indicators. The dataset includes 1,014 records with six features Age, Systolic Blood Pressure, Diastolic Blood Pressure, Blood Sugar, Body Temperature, and Heart Rate, classified into three ordered categories: low, mid, and high risk. The framework uses balanced class weighting as a contoured proxy, ordinal encoding, SHAP-based interpretability, along with probability calibration and conformal prediction to enhance reliability and transparency. Multiple algorithms, including Logistic Regression, Support Vector Machine, Random Forest, and Ordinal Gradient Boosting, were developed and compared under cross-validation. Through this approach, we aim to bridge the gap between statistical accuracy and clinical applicability by ensuring that the model’s predictions align with both ordinal severity and practical decision-making needs. The result shows a clinically interpretable and cost-aware decision-support framework that can assist healthcare providers in improving maternal risk assessment and preventive care. © (2026) by Institute of Industrial & Systems Engineers (IISE) All rights reserved.
