TI  - Enhancing Driver Safety with an Ensemble Deep Framework (EDF) for Distracted Driving Detection
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Next Generation Computing Systems: Intelligent System for Sustainable Development, ICNGCS 2025 - Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/ICNGCS64900.2025.11183272
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021994273?origin=resultslist
AB  - The World Health Organization (WHO) reports that driver distraction constitutes one of the major reasons for vehicular accidents. While driving a vehicle, drivers often do secondary tasks which divert driver's attention. Such tasks include conversation with passengers, utilizing devices such as smartphones or tablets, consuming food or beverages, and so on. In an effort to mitigate these distractions, numerous researchers are concentrating on the development of a model or system that employs machine learning and deep learning methodologies to accurately identify distracted drivers. Nevertheless, several of these methodologies are prone to misclassification errors, experience overfitting, and fail to yield satisfactory outcomes. To rectify these challenges, an Ensemble Deep Framework (EDF) has been proposed, which is an enhanced variant of the ResNet model, incorporating a capsule layer positioned between the fully connected layer and the pooling layer. The model is trained on the dataset. Each image of dataset undergoes image resizing and are subsequently employed for training the proposed architectures. Both architectures (ResNet-50 and EDF) are executed, and the results are compared in relation to evaluation metrics, specifically accuracy and confusion matrix. Accuracy rates of 91.5% and 89% are attained with the EDF and ResNet-50 models respectively, indicating that the EDF achieves superior efficiency and has the potential to mitigate the death rates and injuries resulting from driver distraction. © 2025 IEEE.
