TI  - Deep Learning Method Based on ResNet50 and CT Scan Imaging to Detect Kidney Diseases
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Electrical, Electronics, and Computer Science with Advance Power Technologies - A Future Trends, ICE2CPT 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICE2CPT66440.2025.11340346
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033353981?origin=resultslist
AB  - The presence of abnormalities in the kidney, which include cysts, stones and tumours, require early detection in order to reduce morbidity and mortality of individuals. CT scan interpretation is usually tedious and subjective and often error-prone making computerized robust diagnosis systems a necessity. In this research paper, a deep learning method with ResNet50 and four classification categories is proposed to label the kidney CT images as cystic, normal, stone, and tumour. A training, validation, and test dataset was formed as the final target consisting of 12,446 images obtained out of an open-source imagery selection. The transfer learning was applied to the model by using ImageNet weights to extract domain specific features. During performance assessment, accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score evaluation metrics were used. The accuracy of the proposed framework was 96% overall and the accuracy of the cyst and the tumour detection was excellent. Misclassifications were also identified mostly between normal and tumour categories that may be further improved. The plots of training and validation displayed the optimal performance at approx. 11th epoch, and training epochs before the 11 th were dropped using early stopping, which was effective preventing overfitting. The findings exhibit the usefulness and effectiveness of using ResNet50 in the classification of kidney disease based on CT scans. The methodology has potential to assist clinicians in utilising faster, more reliable diagnoses, with future work involving an increase in collection size and the robustness with hybrid methods. © 2025 IEEE.
