TI  - Optimized ResNet50-Based Framework for Diagnostic Accuracy in Thoracic Radiograph Interpretation
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 1st International Conference on Advancement in Futuristic Technologies, ICAFT 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICAFT66710.2025.11452813
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037329031?origin=resultslist
AB  - Diseases of the lung such as pneumonia and opacities are among the major causes of morbidity and mortality globally. The diagnosis of these conditions is commonly performed using chest X-ray imaging, but since manual analysis may be time-consuming and prone to errors, automated methods are increasingly desired. To classify lung diseases into three categories, namely, Normal, Lung Opacity and Viral Pneumonia, this paper will suggest a refined ResNet50 model. The data, which is downloaded on the open-source will be presented on Kaggle, are 3,475 chest X-ray images spread among the three classes. Preprocessing (image resizing, image normalization, image augmentation) was used to enhance the model robustness. Instead, the ResNet50 backbone served as a feature extractor, and further fully connected, batch normalization, dropout, and softmax layers were added to improve classification. The model had a total accuracy of 96% and equal precision, recall and F1-scores across all categories. Analysis of the confusion matrix showed a high recognition rate, with few misclassifications between Lung Opacity and Viral Pneumonia. Effective convergence with no overfitting was verified with training and validation curves. These results indicate that the fine-tuned ResNet50 is a strong and effective framework to perform automated lung disease detection, and it has a great potential as a computer-aided diagnostic system to assist radiologists, as well as to improve clinical decision-making. © 2025 IEEE.
