UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035279402?origin=resultslist
AB  - Coronary artery disease (CAD) remains a leading cause of global mortality. Stacked‐ensemble machine learning (ML) models trained on structured clinical, laboratory, and echocardiographic measurements can achieve baseline accuracies of 93.26â€¯% on the Cleveland dataset, 90.12â€¯% on Statlog, 90.15â€¯% on Kaggle Framingham, 95.56â€¯% on Z Alizadeh Sani, and 89.06â€¯% on the in house JIPMER cohort. Pre model enhancements like feature evaluation (significant‐feature selection, differential analysis, normalization) and error‐reduction methods (cost‐sensitive learning, manual weight adjustment, precision–recall thresholding, engineered features) raise performance to at most 92.19â€¯% on JIPMER dataset, yet false negatives persist. Active learning (AL) addresses both label scarcity and misclassification risk by iteratively querying the most informative unlabeled cases for expert annotation. We present an AL framework built on a five-model stacked ensemble (Linearâ€¯SVM, RBFâ€¯SVM, KNN, Random Forest, AdaBoost), applied to in house JIPMER and four different public datasets. Our model boosted accuracy to 97.66â€¯% on the JIPMER dataset and eliminated all false negatives using an AL-enhanced stacked ensemble. These results demonstrate that AL can substantially boost diagnostic reliability and cost‐effectiveness of ML‐based CAD prediction tools, supporting rapid clinical deployment in resource constrained settings. © 2026 CRC Press.
