DO  - 10.1109/ICIPTM69057.2026.11466002
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037847257?origin=resultslist
AB  - Identification of mosquito vectors and their proper use to prevent and control the outbreak of Zika virus at an early stage is critical. Traditional morphological identification techniques are however time-consuming, entail expert entomologists and in the field, use damaged specimens and complicated backgrounds. In the present paper, we suggest a computerassisted deep learning-based ensemble system to classify mosquito species with a particular emphasis on the identification of Zika vectors. Two publicly available datasets of real-world and laboratory mosquito images were combined to create a hybrid dataset with 25,912 images of 10 species of mosquitoes. Class balancing technique was class-wise to reduce the extreme class imbalance and increase the learning strength. Using a weighted ensemble strategy to combine two transfer learning models with complementary feature representations, the VGG16 and DenseNet121 were individually optimized and combined together. It has been experimentally shown that the proposed ensemble model has a multi-class classification of 92.43% and a binary Zika vector detection of 99.46% accuracy, being more effective compared to single models and current methods. The confusion matrices and performance per-class analysis confirm that the ensemble greatly decreases the misclassification between different intragenus, but does not decrease the recall of Zika vectors species. The suggested framework promises an effective, scalable, and affordable solution to real-time surveillance of mosquito vectors and can assist the authorities of health of the population to plan the early assessment of the risk of outbreak and control of the vectors. © 2026 IEEE.
