TI  - Accurate Lung Cancer Detection Using Novel Deep Learning Models
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of International Conference on Next-Gen Quantum and Advanced Computing: Algorithms, Security, and Beyond, NQComp 2026
SP  - 75
EP  - 79
DO  - 10.1109/NQComp68334.2026.11497730
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041548620?origin=resultslist
AB  - Lung cancer remains a leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, with patient outcomes heavily dependent on early and accurate diagnosis. While deep learning offers significant potential for automating the analysis of CT scans, model performance is critically dependent on the often-overlooked stages of data preparation and preprocessing. This study presents a comprehensive deep learning pipeline designed to address this gap. The primary objective was to develop and benchmark a robust methodology for the classification of lung cancer from CT scan images, emphasizing the foundational importance of data auditing, specialized preprocessing, and rigorous model evaluation. A systematically curated dataset of CT scan images was utilized, comprising four classes: adenocarcinoma, large cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and normal cases. The methodology is built upon a multi-stage pipeline. First, an extensive exploratory data analysis is conducted, including class distribution statistics and semantic analysis of labels. Second, all images underwent a dedicated preprocessing chain involving Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization (CLAHE) and region-of-interest segmentation to isolate lung fields. Third, the training set was expanded using a tailored data augmentation strategy employing medically plausible transformations. Three convolutional neural network architectures - a custom CNN, a pre-trained ResNet-18, and a pre-trained MobileNetV2 - were trained and evaluated on a held-out validation set. Performance was assessed through accuracy, loss curves, confusion matrices, and detailed classification reports. The fine-tuned pre-trained models achieved superior performance, with MobileNetV2 attaining the highest validation accuracy of 96.82%, followed by ResNet-18 at 96.49% and the custom CNN at 94.78%. All models demonstrated a pronounced ability to identify normal cases with near-perfect precision and recall (0.99-1.00). The most common misclassification occurred between adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma subtypes, reflecting a known diagnostic challenge in clinical practice. The results conclusively demonstrate that a methodical approach to data preparation is a critical determinant of success in medical image analysis. The proposed pipeline provides an effective, reproducible framework for building reliable automated diagnostic aids. The high accuracy achieved, particularly in distinguishing normal from malignant cases, underscores the potential for integrating such systems into clinical workflows to serve as a valuable second reader, thereby enhancing early detection capabilities and supporting diagnostic decision-making. © 2026 IEEE.
