TI  - Correction Correction to: Pemphigoid gestationis is associated with an increased risk for adverse pregnancy outcomes: A large-scale propensity-matched retrospective cohort study (Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology, (2024), 91, 4, (748-750), (S0190962224009551), (10.1016/j.jaad.2024.05.087))
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology
VL  - 94
IS  - 5
SP  - 1614
EP  - 1616
DO  - 10.1016/j.jaad.2026.01.007
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105029251476?origin=resultslist
AB  - Correction to Pemphigoid gestationis is associated with an increased risk for adverse pregnancy outcomes: A large-scale, propensity-matched retrospective cohort study [Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology 2024 Oct;91(4)748-750] Sophie L. Preuß,1 MD, Artem Vorobyev,1 MD, Eva Lotta Moderegger,1 MD, Patrick Terheyden,1 MD, Katja Bieber,2 PhD, Khalaf Kridin,2-4 MD PhD, Pascal Joly,5,6 MD PhD, Ralf J. Ludwig,1,2 MD 1Department of Dermatology, University Medical Center of the State of Schleswig-Holstein (UKSH), Campus Lübeck, Lübeck, Germany 2Lübeck Institute of Experimental Dermatology, University of Lübeck, Lübeck, Germany 3Azrieli Faculty of Medicine, Bar-Ilan University, Safed, Israel 4Unit of Dermatology and Skin Research Laboratory, Barch Padeh Medical Center, Poriya, Israel 5Department of Dermatology, Rouen University Hospital, Normandie University, Rouen, France 6INSERM Unit U1234, PANTHER, Normandie University, Rouen, France The authors have identified an error in the case selection process for their article, “Pemphigoid gestationis is associated with an increased risk for adverse pregnancy outcomes: A large-scale, propensity-matched retrospective cohort study”.1 In TriNetX, data are represented by ICD-10-CM codes, and there is automatically an ICD-9-to-10-CM mapping done based on General Equivalence Mappings plus custom algorithms and curation to transform data to ICD-10-CM by TriNetX. In this case, the database had by default mapped the ICD-9-CM codes 646.81 (other specified complications of pregnancy, delivered with or without mention of antepartum condition) and 646.83 (other specified complications of pregnancy, antepartum condition or complication) to the ICD-10-CM code O26.4 (PG). These ICD-9-CM codes were nonspecific obstetric complication codes, rarely used for true PG, and their inclusion may have introduced exposure misclassification bias, potentially distorting the observed associations in the original analysis. The authors caught this error when starting a follow-up project on PG in the database. The authors performed the analysis again by excluding ICD-9-CM 646.81 and 646.83 from ICD-10-CM O26.4. Additional pregnancy-indicating diagnoses were employed to identify as many pregnant women as possible with this code. The authors have found that the revised analysis continues to demonstrate a significantly increased risk for any adverse pregnancy outcome (APO), as well as increased risks for preterm labor, gestational hypertension without significant proteinuria, preeclampsia and gestational diabetes in patients with PG compared to controls. The previously reported associations with HELLP syndrome and eclampsia were no longer statistically significant. The authors sincerely apologize for this error and any inconvenience it may have caused. The repeated analysis performed at a later timepoint confirmed the robustness of our results and further substantiated the association between PG and APOs. In line with recent discussions on the methodological challenges of database research, it is essential that investigators remain aware of the structural limitations of automated data mapping and other inherent biases in real-world data sources. These aspects should be carefully considered in future studies, ideally guided by the best practice bundle recently outlined in the Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology.2,3 The following revised paragraphs, along with a revised Table I and Fig 1, should be considered the updated versions of their original counterparts in order to reflect corrections from the revised analysis. “Women aged 12 to 55 were identified, and baseline defined by initial coding of diagnoses indicating pregnancy by the following codes of International Classification of Diseases, Tenth Revision, Clinical Modification (ICD-10-CM) or Procedure Coding System (ICD10PCS) codes and Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) codes: ICD10CM:Z32.01 (encounter for pregnancy test, result positive), ICD10CM:Z32.2 (encounter for childbirth instruction), ICD10CM:Z33 (pregnant state), ICD10CM:Z34 (encounter for supervision of a normal pregnancy), ICD10CM:Z36 (encounter for antenatal screening of mother), ICD10CM:Z3A (weeks of gestation), ICD10CM:Z03.7 (encounter for suspected maternal and fetal conditions ruled out), CPT:1010785 (diagnostic ultrasound procedures of the pelvis obstetrical), ICD10PCS:BY (fetus and obestrical) or ICD10PCS:10 (pregnancy). In this cohort, PG was defined by ICD-10-CM: O26.4 and automated ICD-9-CM mapping of ICD-9-CM 646.81 and 646.83 was excluded. Controls were defined by the absence of ICD-10-CM: O26.4.” “The study population included 4.024 propensity-matched electronic health records in both groups (Table 1). PG conferred a higher risk of preterm labor (HR: 1.5, 95% confidence interval [CI]: 1.2-1.8, P < .0001), gestational hypertension without significant proteinuria (HR: 2.5, CI: 2.1-2.8, P < .0001), preeclampsia (HR: 2.3, CI: 1.9-2.8, P < .0001), gestational diabetes (HR: 1.3, CI: 1.1-1.6, P = .0021), and any of the analyzed APOs (HR: 1.7, CI: 1.6-1.9, P < .0001). A decreased risk for spontaneous abortion was observed (HR: 0.3, CI: 0.2-0.4, P < .0001). There was no significantly higher risk for hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet count syndrome (HR: 0.7, CI: 0.3-1.4, P = .2821) and eclampsia (HR: 0.9, CI: 0.4-1.9, P = .7924) observed (Fig 1, Supplementary Table 2 available via Mendeley athttps://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wtn2phppdk/2).” © 2026 American Academy of Dermatology, Inc.
