TI  - Early Breast Cancer Detection System Using Explainable Attention-Based Deep Learning Framework
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Intelligent and Sustainable Electronics and Computing Technologies, INSECT 2026
DO  - 10.1109/INSECT68872.2026.11663675
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051544797?origin=resultslist
AB  - Breast cancer is the most prevalent type of cancer found in women worldwide and a leading cause of death globally, so an accurate diagnosis at an earlier time can increase survival. Mammography, in addition to ultrasound and magnetic resonance imaging, are common diagnostic methods used to detect irregular tissue within the breast. But these approaches depend heavily on an expert interpretation that may lead to misclassification or indeed a late-stage diagnosis. Deep Learning (DL) techniques continue to yield impressive results in interpreting computer vision and medical images, such as classifying different diseases automatically with high accuracy. In this study, we designed deep learning with an explainable attention framework for breast cancer early detection system. We proposed a classification framework including image preprocessing, feature extraction by Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), and an extensive attention mechanism to provide more importance to the significant regions in medical images. In addition, an explainability module is included to enhance the interpretability of model outputs. This system proposed to classify breast tumors into benign and malignant. The experimental findings indicate that these three deep learning methodologies outperform machine learning models F1 score, recall and precision.  © 2026 IEEE.
