TI  - Lightweight CNN-based Malaria detection framework based on the use of MobileNetV2
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Conference on NexGen Networks and Cybernetics, IC2NC 2025 - Proceedings
SP  - 204
EP  - 209
DO  - 10.1109/IC2NC67409.2025.11376346
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035617922?origin=resultslist
AB  - Malaria, mainly caused by Plasmodium parasites which is transmitted by the bite of infected mosquitoes, is considered one of the most life-threatening illnesses in the tropical and sub-tropical areas. The mortality rates can be reduced through early and precise detection of malaria by facilitating timely treatment. The proposed study is an effective deep learning model that makes use of a MobileNetV2-based Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to identify malaria using image-based microscopic blood smears. Extracted data comes in the form of 550 images in two classes; Parasitized (311 images) and Uninfected (239 images) and is sourced out of Kaggle. The series of observation is found to classify with an overall accuracy of 94% which showed a high generalization and reliability of the proposed model. The Parasitized group attained a precision of 0.93 and a recall of 0.99 whereas the Uninfected group obtained a precision of 0.97 and recall of 0.84. The convergence of the training and validation curves is good and there is no overfitting as evidenced by the confusion matrix which indicate that the prediction is correct with a limited number of misclassifications. The combination of lightweight architecture and transfer learning greatly streamlined the level of computation and is thus applicable in the resource constrained healthcare setup. This model contributes to the sustainable development by facilitating high standards of medical diagnostics, enhancing better health results, healthcare advancement, and enhancing international collaboration in combating contagious diseases. © 2025 IEEE.
