TI  - Modality - Agnostic Deep Learning for Pneumonia and COVID-19 Detection Across Chest X-Ray, CT, and Lung Ultrasound
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Conference on AI-Driven Innovations and Applications, IC-AIDA 2026 - Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/IC-AIDA68291.2026.11564373
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044302315?origin=resultslist
AB  - Pneumonia continues to be one of the leading causes of morbidity and mortality worldwide, and timely diagnosis is critical for effective treatment. The urgency of accurate detection was further intensified during the COVID-19 pandemic. This paper presents a deep learning framework that utilizes chest X-ray, computed tomography, and lung ultrasound images within a unified convolutional neural network designed to function independently of imaging modality. Instead of applying feature-level fusion, the model is trained on a harmonized dataset of heterogeneous images, which enables generalization across different imaging conditions. Computed tomography provides superior diagnostic sensitivity due to its high anatomical resolution, while chest X-ray and ultrasound remain widely accessible, portable, and safer for repeated use. Experimental evaluation shows that the proposed system achieves 98.2 percent accuracy on chest X-ray using ResNet50, 98.7 percent on computed tomography using DenseNet121, and 75 percent on ultrasound using EfficientNetB0. The MedNetLite multimodal model attained 97.1 percent accuracy, demonstrating consistent performance across diverse imaging inputs and reducing misclassification in difficult pneumonia and COVID-19 cases. These results confirm that each modality contributes unique diagnostic value, while the unified model learns generalizable discriminative features. The proposed framework provides a scalable and adaptable solution for clinical environments where imaging resources and conditions may vary. © 2026 IEEE.
