TI  - AI-Powered Lung Cancer Detection: CNN-SVM Hybrid Model with Statistical Insights
PY  - 2025
T2  - 3rd International Conference on Electronics and Renewable Systems, ICEARS 2025 - Proceedings
SP  - 1651
EP  - 1655
DO  - 10.1109/ICEARS64219.2025.10940698
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105002724098?origin=resultslist
AB  - Liability of lung cancer as a primary cancer mortality agent around the world makes it vital to create diagnostic systems that guarantee both accuracy and operational efficiency. This investigation establishes a hybrid detection methodology which uses CNNs for extracting image features from CT scans alongside SVMs for classification to improve lung cancer identification performance. The combined CNN-SVM model attained top performance with 95.2% accuracy surpassing the 91.5% CNN model correctness and 88.3% correctness from the SVM model. The sensitivity of the hybrid model reached 94.8%, and its specificity was 95.6%, demonstrating superior performance in correctly identifying malignant and benign cases compared to CNN (sensitivity: 90.2%, specificity: 92.8%) and SVM (sensitivity: 87.9%, specificity: 88.7%). With an AUC of 0.98 CNN-SVM demonstrated superior discriminative capability than CNN's model at 0.94 and SVM's model at 0.91. The confusion matrix analysis validated this model because it correctly determined 476 malignant and 478 benign cases among 500 total cases resulting in 2.4% misclassification. Analysis of both training and validation loss curves exhibited proper convergence with insignificant overfitting because readings stabilized at 0.05 for training and 0.08 for validation data. The hybrid model demonstrates improved lung cancer detection which validates its medical application potential as an effective solution. Further research will stretch dataset boundaries while adding multi-class categorization and exploring clearer results through explainable AI methods. © 2025 IEEE.
