TI  - HistAdvNet: A robust deep learning framework for histopathology classification under adversarial conditions
PY  - 2026
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
VL  - 125
C7  - 110760
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2026.110760
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041384717?origin=resultslist
AB  - Accurate classification is critical in automated cancer diagnosis, where lung and colon cancers (LCC) rank among the most prevalent and deadliest malignancies worldwide, collectively accounting for millions of fatalities each year. Lung cancer remains the leading cause of cancer-related deaths globally, while colon cancer is among the top three in terms of both incidence and mortality. Despite advancements in treatment, delayed and inaccurate diagnoses continue to hinder patient outcomes and burden healthcare systems. Deep learning models have shown great promise in improving diagnostic precision, but they remain vulnerable to adversarial perturbation, subtle modifications that can lead to misclassification and pose serious clinical risks. The major issue is to construct adversarially robust and clinically interpretable deep learning frameworks that can generalize across numerous datasets and real-world heterogeneity. In this work, we introduce HistAdvNet, a deep learning framework designed to enhance both classification accuracy and adversarial robustness in multi-class histopathological image (HI) datasets. The framework integrates architectural innovations with strategically developed training protocols to defend against adversarial attacks. Through this synergy, HistAdvNet demonstrates significant improvements in performance and Adversarial robustness across a range of adversarial scenarios. For comprehensive evaluation, multiple pretrained models, alongside a custom lightweight deep learning architecture, were assessed using the HI image dataset. We evaluate our framework using the HI dataset and employ several adversarial attacks to assess robustness. Experimental results revealed that all models, including the custom CNN framework, achieved high predictive accuracy of 97±02 on clean datasets, with minimal loss. Under adversarial stress, retained accuracies ranged from ∼60% to ∼79%, highlighting the vulnerability of conventional architectures, whereas HistAdvNet maintained an outstanding prediction accuracy of nearly 98.4%. Despite HistAdvNet's demonstrated clinical deployment viability and potential to enhance early cancer diagnosis worldwide, challenges, including dataset variability, residual adversarial sensitivity, and the demand for standardized clinical interpretability, still need to be addressed. © 2026
