TI  - Cost Sensitive Ordinal Classification for Meningitis Risk Stratification Considering Clinical Aspects to Minimize False Negatives
PY  - 2026
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 289
SP  - 996
EP  - 1005
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2026.08.319
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052772560?origin=resultslist
AB  - Meningitis is a life-threatening disease where delaying the identification of high-risk patients leads to severe clinical outcomes and increased mortality. Conventional machine learning for risk prediction usually treat outcome categories as independent classes, which ignore the ordinal nature of clinical severity. As a result, these approach does not penalize significant misclassifications well, especially false negatives in high-risk cases. This study suggest a cost-sensitive ordinal classification system for meningitis risk stratification using clinical and demographic data in order to overcome this drawback. This framework integrate modeling that considers the ordinal nature of the target. A higher penalty is applied to high-risk misclassifications using a penalty matrix. We evaluate the model performance by proposing a custom weighted aggregated standard metrics (i.e. accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score) prioritizing recall in order to improve clinical safety and Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC). Custom clinical penalty and Mean Squared Error (MSE) are used to evaluate ordinal perspective. Cost sensitive Random Forest is the best model with a weighted score of 0.834 and an MCC of 0.821 when compared to the baseline multinominal model, ordinal logistic regression model, and other cost sensitive models, including LightGBM, and XGBoost. This performance outperforms the baseline multiclass model, which achieves a score of 0.791 and an MCC of 0.750, and shows an improvement over the worst-performing ordinal logistic regression model, which obtains a score of 0.654 and an MCC of 0.673. These findings underscore the value of incorporating ordinal structure and cost-sensitive learning into the model. This study uses a reliable framework for early intervention and helps hospital decision making. © 2026 The Author(s).
