TI  - Quantifying the Safety Versus Speed Trade-Off of the DeepSeqCoco Framework for Dermatological Classification via a Two-Stage Stability Evaluation Protocol
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Access
VL  - 14
SP  - 86738
EP  - 86763
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3700866
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041984137?origin=resultslist
AB  - Skin diseases are a major health problem for the whole of humanity, with limited access to timely and accurate diagnostic tools, especially in resource-constricted areas. While AI-assisted diagnostic support systems show promise, existing solutions tend to be computationally expensive or lack rigorous validation for sensitivity and edge deployment feasibility. To address this gap, we propose the Two-Stage Stability (TSS) Protocol to rigorously evaluate the cross-domain portability of the DeepSeqCoco framework for mobile-centric dermatological classification. Unlike standard point-estimate studies, TSS explicitly quantifies initialization variance, INT8 quantization degradation, and a novel 'Clinical Safety Penalty' for life-threatening misclassifications. Stage 1 optimization screening identified a Hybrid SGD Adam strategy as optimal. In Stage 2, multi-seed (N=5 ) evaluations verified by Welch's t-test (p < 0.001 ) and McNemar's test revealed a strict Pareto trade-off between edge speed and diagnostic safety. The EfficientNetB3 configuration achieved a highly stable accuracy of 94.66% (Coefficient of Variation: 0.0053) and a Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC) of 0.944, acting as an optimal cloud-based confirmatory tool. However, its massive 10.79M parameter space restricted TFLite inference to ~3.7 FPS. Conversely, MobileNetV3-Small achieved 91.03% accuracy and enabled real-time edge screening at ~114 FPS. Crucially, we quantify the hidden clinical toll of lightweight architectures: MobileNetV3-Small missed an average of 46.2 cases of Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS-TEN) compared to EfficientNetB3's 22.0. To safely harness edge efficiency without compromising patient mortality, we propose a Two-Tier Triage Strategy, balancing local computational speed with high-parameter cloud confirmation. © 2013 IEEE.
