TI  - Photonic Crystal Biosensor for Classifying Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer Subtypes using Refractive Index Variation and Glorot-Initialized Artificial Neural Network
PY  - 2026
T2  - 4th International Conference on Integrated Circuits and Communication Systems, ICICACS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICICACS68679.2026.11578562
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044430948?origin=resultslist
AB  - The classification of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) subtypes, such as Lung Adenocarcinoma (LUAD) and Lung Squamous Cell carcinoma (LUSC), is helps to implement the targeted therapies and reducing high mortality rates. Due to their dependency on visual color features and texture, existing Deep Learning (DL) models such as transformers fail to distinguish these subtypes, leading to misclassification. This paper proposes a Photonic Crystal sensor-based Glorot-initialized Artificial Neural Network (PhC-GANN) to address these challenges by leveraging numerical data from a Two-Dimensional (2D) photonic crystal sensor, which provides minute variations in subtypes. The model employs an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) suitable for sensor data that can extract the complex, non-linear characteristics of the Refractive Index (RI). Additionally, Glorot (Xavier) initialization is applied to maintain consistent activation and signal variations through the deep layers of the ANN model. These techniques balance signal magnitudes during both forward and backward passes, overcoming signal attenuation. This allows the model to detect minute shifts in resonance wavelength by providing a reliable numerical alternative to image-based features. The proposed PhC-GANN achieves 99.71% accuracy on the sensor data, demonstrating its effectiveness in accurately classifying NSCLS subtypes by differentiating overlapping classes. © 2026 IEEE.
