TI  - Classification of Kidney Disease from CT Images: A Deep Learning-Based Comparative Analysis
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceeding - 2025 IEEE 9th International Conference on Software Engineering and Computer Systems: Advancements in Next-Generation Intelligent Solution, ICSECS 2025
SP  - 353
EP  - 358
DO  - 10.1109/ICSECS65227.2025.11279151
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032640531?origin=resultslist
AB  - Kidney disease is a social and global health concern where early detection is most important to reduce mortality. Traditional diagnostic methods require significant amount of time and may often give inaccurate results which can be prone to human error. This study aims to build an efficient automated diagnostic system for kidney disease detection using medical imaging. A deep learning framework is proposed on this study that combines the features of two state-of-the-art convolutional neural networks DenseNet121 and EfficientNetB0 for robust feature extraction. These extracted features are then classified using an ensemble of Support Vector Machine (SVM), Random Forest (RF), and XGBoost combined by soft voting. A total of 12,447 CT images were used and the test images were 1869. The proposed model has been able to predict 1850 images correctly, and unfortunately 19 cases are misclassified from various kidney cases, cysts, stones, tumors, and normal. It has achieved outstanding performance metrics with an accuracy of 98.98% and F1-score of 98.60%. The proposed model enables early and accurate detection of kidney disease, aiding timely treatment, especially in resource-limited settings. Its explainable AI approach enhances trust and supports scalable, accessible healthcare solutions. ©2025 IEEE.
