TI  - Federated Reinforcement Learning-Enhanced Swin Transformer V2 for Reducing Diagnostic Errors in Lung Nodule Analysis
PY  - 2026
T2  - Conference Proceedngs - WcCST 2026: World Conference on Computational Science and Technology
SP  - 833
EP  - 839
DO  - 10.1109/WcCST67302.2026.11495694
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040795192?origin=resultslist
AB  - Early and accurate lung nodule detection is essential for reducing lung cancer mortality; however, diagnostic errors persist due to variability in nodule size, shape, appearance, and imaging quality across diverse clinical settings. Existing centralized deep learning models, including CNNs and transformer-based approaches, often face limited generalization, sensitivity to noise, and data privacy concerns, resulting in false positives and false negatives. These limitations highlight the need for adaptive, privacy-preserving, and robust learning frameworks suitable for distributed medical data. This study presents a Federated Reinforcement Learning-Enhanced Swin Transformer V2 (FRL-SwinV2) framework for reliable lung nodule analysis using chest CT images. The proposed approach employs SwinV2 as a hierarchical backbone to capture multi-scale visual features, while Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training across institutions without sharing raw patient data. A RL agent is incorporated into the federated process to dynamically optimize model parameters, attention window configurations, and decision thresholds using reward-based feedback, allowing the system to adaptively learn from misclassified and ambiguous nodules. The primary objective is to enhance diagnostic accuracy while reducing false detections and preserving patient privacy. Experimental results show notable improvements in accuracy, sensitivity, and AUC along with reduced error rates compared to conventional CNN and standalone transformer models. Overall, FRL-SwinV2 offers a robust, scalable, and privacy-aware solution for error-prone lung nodule detection in real-world clinical environments. © 2026 IEEE.
