TI  - Detection of lung nodules from the CT images with Border Collie Optimization based DCNN classifier
PY  - 2024
T2  - 2024 International Conference on Recent Innovation in Smart and Sustainable Technology, ICRISST 2024
DO  - 10.1109/ICRISST59181.2024.10921831
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105001664793?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cancer, one of the chronic diseases, is the major cause of death at its later stages. Among the various cancer types, lung cancer accounts for the two-third fatal rate which can be identified with the changes observed in the lung nodules. The early detection of these changes can increase the survivability and the detection methods such as imaging modalities help the radiologist to predict manually. This manual prediction sometimes leads to misconceptions, which can change the entire treatment procedure. In order to enhance prediction and survival rate, deep learning methods are implanted in the medical environment in which former methods provide outcomes with certain drawbacks such as less robustness, misclassification, and so on. To provide better lung nodule detection (LND) from computed tomography (CT) images, the optimized Deep Convolutional Neural network (DCNN) model is proposed. The model is optimized with Border collie optimization (BCO) which helps to enhance the classification performance. The features of the model are extracted with the gray level co-occurrence matrix (GLCM) features and VGG-16 based features along with the Centralized Superpixels Segmentation Iterative Clustering (CSSBIC) segmentation that provides enhanced accuracy. The accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity are the performance metrics utilized to evaluate the performance of the BCO optimized DCNN that achieves 92.78 %, 96.64%, and 88.12% respectively. © 2024 IEEE.
