TI  - Providing Cognitive Platforms for the Effectiveness of Cancer Vaccines
PY  - 2024
T2  - Cancer Vaccination and Challenges: Volume 1: Strategies for Therapeutic Cancer Vaccine Development
SP  - 293
EP  - 322
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85207998866?origin=resultslist
AB  - Population growth in recent years has led to an increase in various diseases. The spread of these diseases, along with their unknown causes, has resulted in delays in providing effective treatments. Cancer, in particular, has become one of the most prevalent and painful diseases in recent years. The incidence of cancer varies based on factors such as gender, age, and geographic conditions. Therefore, the development of an intelligent system capable of diagnosing and preventing cancer can significantly reduce the occurrence of this disease and decrease mortality rates. Furthermore, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) have made a significant impact on the field of medicine. By utilizing ML techniques, it becomes possible to diagnose specific symptoms experienced by individuals and predict the type of cancer they may be suffering from. Consequently, the accurate diagnosis of various cancerous tumors using ML algorithms can expedite patient treatment. In fact, cancerous tumors are examined through image processing, followed by detailed recognition using deep learning techniques. With the use of intelligent tools, such as smart watches, suspicious symptoms can be identified, enabling faster treatment for individuals. Smart applications are highly beneficial for all age groups, particularly the elderly who may struggle with accurately diagnosing symptoms. However, despite these advancements, the importance of vaccination cannot be overlooked. Vaccination is a common method to prevent the spread of diseases, effectively reducing mortality rates. Encouraging the use of vaccines can prevent diseases and promote overall health within society. To prepare vaccines, it is crucial to explore the existing challenges and problems. This involves identifying disease symptoms in individuals and selecting vaccines that do not exacerbate other conditions. Smart medical applications can enhance the diagnosis of different disease symptoms, ensuring accurate identification of patients’ problems and minimizing diagnostic errors. Additionally, the use of artificial intelligence technologies in vaccine design can streamline the production process, reducing the time required for each stage. In this chapter, we will examine various types of cancers, explore methods for designing and producing vaccines tailored to each cancer, and investigate the challenges and opportunities associated with utilizing artificial intelligence technologies for future cancer vaccine (CV) preparation. © 2025 Apple Academic Press, Inc.
