TI  - PATTERN OF SUDDEN NATURAL DEATHS AMONG AUTOPSIES CONDUCTED AT KIMS HOSPITAL BENGALURU.
PY  - 2023
T2  - Journal of South India Medicolegal Association
VL  - 15
IS  - 2
SP  - 61
EP  - 64
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85178958953?origin=resultslist
AB  - Background Natural deaths are inevitable but sudden natural deaths important from a medico legal point as they may raise suspicion of foul play. Hence brought for autopsy. Sudden death is a death which is not known to have been caused by any trauma, poisoning or violent asphyxia, and where death occurs all of a sudden or within 24 hours of the onset of the terminal symptoms. Aims To find the socio-demographic profile such as age, sex of the deceased of sudden death and to find out the pattern of cause of sudden natural deaths over a specified period in a particular geographical area. Materials and Methodology This cross-sectional retrospective study was conducted in the department of Forensic Medicine, KIMS, Bengaluru, from June 2021 to December 2021. During this period there were 176 cases of sudden natural death. Results During the period of study, autopsy was conducted on 176 sudden natural deaths, of which, were male. Majority of the cases were from the 25–50 year age group, minimum from the age group of up to 10 years. Of the 176 cases, 110 were due to diseases of cardiovascular system, 40 due to diseases of respiratory,, 4 due to diseases of liver, 6 due to unknown cause respectively,. Coronary artery disease was the single largest cause of death, accounting for 110 deaths (63%). The other causes of death in the descending order of frequency were: Lobar Pneumonia (40 cases) combined liver and CF (20 cases). Majority of these deaths cases occurred while the person was outside the hospital setting (64%), followed by patients who were admitted to the hospital (36%). Among the study population, 49% were Hindu by religion. Conclusion We found that a significant number of sudden deaths occurred in adults, particularly in the population above 30years, posing a health concern in our society. Cardiac causes contributed the maximum number, with atherosclerosis being the main culprit in causing CAD. © 2023 South India Medico-Legal Association. All rights reserved.
