AB  - For ordinal outcomes, the average treatment effect is often ill-defined and hard to interpret. Echoing Agresti and Kateri, we argue that the relative treatment effect can be a useful measure, especially for ordinal outcomes, which is defined as (Formula presented.), with (Formula presented.) and (Formula presented.) being the potential outcomes of unit (Formula presented.) under treatment and control, respectively. Given the marginal distributions of the potential outcomes, we derive the sharp bounds on (Formula presented.) which are identifiable parameters based on the observed data. Agresti and Kateri focused on modeling strategies under the assumption of independent potential outcomes, but we allow for arbitrary dependence. © 2019 The International Biometric Society
