TI  - Fine particulate PM2.5 air pollution and respiratory mortality in Allegheny County, PA
PY  - 2017
T2  - Proceedings of the Air and Waste Management Association's Annual Conference and Exhibition, AWMA
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85039153192?origin=resultslist
AB  - Background: We evaluated the acute effects of fine particulate matter (PM2.5) on respiratory mortality among residents of Allegheny County in SW Pennsylvania (1999-2011) using spatio-temporal modeling of air pollutants (AP) to reduce misclassification error in exposure assessment. Methods: Spatio-temporal kriging of daily PM2.5 and ozone (O3) was used to produce daily exposure estimates at the residence ZIP code. Time-stratified case-crossover analysis was conducted to examine short-term effects of PM2.5 on respiratory mortality, adjusting for O3 and apparent mean temperature. We studied both single and distributed lags for days 0-5. All-cause respiratory mortality and subcategories of deaths due to pneumonia and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) were studied. Results: A total of 15,747 respiratory disease deaths were identified. We found significant associations of PM2.5 in both single and two pollutant models (with ozone) with total respiratory disease mortality at lag day 4 in distributed lag models - a 7.0% (95% CI, 2%-12%) increase per 10 ìg/m3 PM2.5 in both of these models. Conclusions: The application of finer scale geographically resolved AP exposures made it possible to study acute effects of PM2.5 on respiratory mortality in a large metropolitan area. Our study results demonstrated the continued presence of a dose response relationship of increased risk of respiratory mortality within this lower range of PM2.5 exposure. We also noted for COPD, for two pollutant models in the summer season, there was a significant effect of ozone for single lag day five; each 10 ppb increment in ozone concentration was associated with an increase of 6.7% in COPD mortality (95% CI, 0.2-13.6%) compared to the cooler months.
