TI  - Automatic tuberculosis detection using adaptive thresholding in chest radiographs
PY  - 2017
T2  - Proceedings of IEEE International Conference on Emerging Technological Trends in Computing, Communications and Electrical Engineering, ICETT 2016
C7  - 7873635
DO  - 10.1109/ICETT.2016.7873635
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85017251756?origin=resultslist
AB  - Tuberculosis is a global health threatening disease with mortality rates increasing day by day. As earlier TB detection methods do not ensure accurate results, there is an immediate action required to detect the disease with proper tools. To address this issue, a novel mechanism is proposed to detect tuberculosis in chest radiographs. The proposed method includes a three stage process of accurate detection of tuberculosis. In the first stage, lung segmentation using adaptive thresholding is utilized to segment the chest images which have different illumination. The second stage of feature extraction modifies the input image into a set of features which extracts relevant information contained in the chest image. In the third stage of process, k-Nearest Neighbor classifier (k-NN) outputs the decision that the input image is normal or abnormal. The detection process is evaluated using four performance analyzing criteria: accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and area under the ROC curve (AUC). Simulation result shows that the accuracy of our proposed system is 4% more than the graph-cut based TB detection system. A comparison task is also performed to measure the quality of classifier among three classifiers: k-NN, support vector machine and artificial neural network classifier. Results show that k-NN classifier outperforms the other two classifiers with much lower misclassification rate. © 2016 IEEE.
