TI  - Evaluation of Potential Confounding by Smoking in the Presence of Misclassified Smoking Data in a Cohort Study of Workers Exposed to Acrylonitrile
PY  - 2015
T2  - Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine
VL  - 57
IS  - 2
SP  - 146
EP  - 151
DO  - 10.1097/JOM.0000000000000386
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84930542099?origin=resultslist
AB  - OBJECTIVES: To evaluate the extent to which lung cancer mortality risk estimates in relation to acrylonitrile (AN) exposure may have been confounded by smoking in the presence of misclassified smoking data. METHODS: Subjects were 992 white men employed for three or more months between 1960 and 1996 at a chemical plant in Lima, Ohio. We used Monte Carlo-based sensitivity analysis to address possible confounding by smoking. RESULTS: In Monte Carlo simulations that accounted for the relationship between smoking and AN exposure, mean relative risks for lung cancer mortality in relation to AN exposure decreased and we observed somewhat less evidence of an exposure-response relationship. CONCLUSIONS: Our simulations suggest that the relationship between AN exposure and lung cancer mortality was positively confounded by smoking in the original Lima cohort study. Copyright © 2015 by the American College of Occupational and Environmental Medicine.
