UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/32544449805?origin=resultslist
AB  - After an external examination of a corpse, the cause of death often remains unclear. Serious problems can arise particularly in cases where the patient dies without medical care, where a doctor who does not know the patient has to perform the external examination, where no case history is known or in cases were a body is found dead under unknown circumstances. In countries such as Austria, Great Britain or Scandinavia autopsies are performed in cases with unknown cause of death, primarily to identify the cause of death. These autopsies are ordered by appropriate authorities such as the coroner (Great Britain) or health departments of the city or county. However, in Germany an equivalent to such autopsies ordered by a department does not exist. Although clinical and forensic pathologists in Germany have claimed coroners autopsies (Verwaltungssektionen) for more than 100 years, the efforts have not been crowned with success. The legal framework which may enable the introduction of coroners autopsies in Germany is discussed. With the introduction of coroners autopsies in Germany, limitations of certifying the cause of death by an external examination could be solved. Coroners autopsies are in the interest of the doctors, the whole healthcare system, the public, the relatives and the deceased. © Springer Medizin Verlag 2005.
