SP  - 145
EP  - 149
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0346460097?origin=resultslist
AB  - Introduction: Diarrhea is one of the most prevalent serious health problems facing infants and children. It is a major cause of morbidity and mortality with a significant health cost especially in developing countries. Chronic diarrhea defined as loose stool lasting fourteen days or more. Objective: The objective of this study is to determine the changes in prevalence, clinical presentation, etiology, and histopathologic pattern of chronic diarrhea patients admitted to MGH between January 1997 and December 2002 in comparison to previous study conducted in MGH between 1987-1991. Methods: 83 medical records were reviewed retrospectively study over a 5 years period (January 1997 and December 2002), patients demographic, clinical and nutritional history, growth parameters and paramedical data were collected. Diarrhea duration ranged between 30 and 190d (mean 147.5). Age ranged between 1 month and 78 months (mean7mo). Birth weight ranged between 1500 and 4000grs (mean 3000grs). Six patients were excluded. Results: According to clinical data, laboratory studies and jejunal biopsies, chronic diarrhea was classified into 5 groups: group I celiac disease (16%), group II infectious gastrointestinal diarrhea (21.3%) The most common cause was intestinal amebiasis group III diarrhea secondary to systemic illness (12%), group IV cow's milk (44%)group V undetermined cause (6.7%). The infectious cause was Entameba coli cyst. Jejunal biopsy done in 66 out of 77 revealed partial atrophy in 7 patients (10.6%), total & subtotal villous atrophy in 17 patients (25.8%), and normotrophic villi in 42 patients (63.6%). 27.8% of patients were on breastteeding while 67.6% were on bottle-feeding. Of all patients, 85% were of low socioeconomic status. Conclusion: Infectious control efforts in Lebanon have showed good results over the past 10 years & should be continued for better level of sanitation & prevention. Reevaluation and reconsideration of all basic programs that supports & encourage breast feeding by using mass media and educational programs. As a specialized referral center for chronic diarrhea an effort should be made for the development of a specialized unit for Total Parenteral Nutrition.
