TI  - Mortality among pacemaker patients. A population-based study in Fyn County, Denmark, 1984-1989
PY  - 1996
T2  - European Journal of Cardiac Pacing and Electrophysiology
VL  - 6
IS  - 2
SP  - 104
EP  - 109
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0029952794?origin=resultslist
AB  - We have evaluated the cause of death in pacemaker patients over the 6 year period 1984-1989 in Fyn County, Denmark (comprising 460,000 inhabitants). The deaths were recorded from a record-linkage between the regional register of all pacemaker patients and the public register. All deaths were grouped according to age at death, gender and cause of death (heart disease, cerebrovascular disease, cancer, violent death, unknown cause, and all other causes). Reference values of expected numbers of death were obtained from age- and sex-specific mortality data from the general Danish population for the same observation period. In total, 379 deaths were observed against 226.5 expected (Standardised Mortality Ratio, SMR = 1.7; P < 0.0001). The relative mortality was highest in the younger age classes. No statistically significant difference between genders was observed and there was no statistical evidence of any secular trends in the mortality during the study period. Significantly increased relative mortality was observed for heart disease (SMR = 2.3 (188/83.4); P < 0.0001), for unknown cause of death (SMR = 3.5 (27/7.7); p < 0.0001), and for the category of all other causes (SMR = 1.9 (106/55.1); p < 0.0001). The relative mortality for death due to cancer was statistically significantly reduced (SMR = 0.6 (28/46.0); p < 0.01). Deaths due to cerebrovascular disease and violent causes occurred as expected. It is concluded that pacemaker patients experience a mortality higher than expected in the general population, and that this excess mortality is predominantly related to heart disease and unknown causes (sudden death under ill-defined circumstances?). The reduced mortality from cancer may reflect a restricted indication for pacemaker implantation in patients with known malignant disease.
