UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0017762449?origin=resultslist
AB  - As a result of HLA antigen studies a large number of mainly chronic diseases of unknown causes have been shown to have a significant genetic component in their aetiology. The majority of these associations seem to be mediated via disease-associated immune-response genes. However, the same HLA antigens influence recovery from hepatitis B antigenaemia and survival in leukaemia and lymphoma. This suggests that they may have evolved because of selective evolutionary advantages at a time when infectious disease was a major cause of death before the age of reproductive maturity. It is important to emphasize that none of these associations has shown a one-to-one relationship between gene and disease. There are two genetic explanations for this, that is the involvement of non-HLA-linked loci in addition to the HLA-associated gene as was shown in gluten enteropathy, and alternatively the involvement of an as yet undefined locus in linkage disequilibrium with antigens of the HLA locus. The latter was shown in the progressively stronger association seen in multiple sclerosis as techniques became available to study the various components of the A3/B7/DW2 haplotype. Because genetic engineering does not offer a practical approach to overcoming these defects, the principal aim of research must be to develop new approaches to understanding the mechanism involved in disease production as has been attempted by Ebringer et al (1976) in their studies of ankylosing spondylitis. However, since some associations are sufficiently strong to be of diagnostic significance the place for HLA typing in routine investigation of patients must also be assessed.
