TI  - Causes of death and a new concept in total artificial heart
PY  - 1974
T2  - Surgical Forum
VL  - VOL. 25
SP  - 137
EP  - 139
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0016163639?origin=resultslist
AB  - Since 10 day survival with the total artifical heart (TAH) has been achieved (1971), experimental results have been remarkably improved, and one 25 day survivor was obtained in 1973. As the survival time was prolonged, causes of death of experimental calves changed and the concept of pathophysiological problems in TAH also changed. Sixty two experiments were performed from March 1971 to May 1974. The animals were divided into 2 groups according to their survival time: those pumped less than 100 hr (37 calves), and those more than 100 hr (25 calves). The causes of death of 20 calves in the first group consisted of technical and mechanical failures such as accident (5 calves), surgical failure (6), breakage of the device (7), and failure of the driving machine (2). Accidents included slippage of the monitoring line in the left mammary artery, slippage of the left air tube inside the left chest cavity, and occlusion of the air tube. Surgical failure was mainly caused by the small chest cavity, in which the device could not be adequately implanted nor could the chest be closed completely. Breakage of the device was seen only on the left side. Breakage of the pumping chamber caused excessive systemic air emboli, and breakage of the housing caused pneumothorax. Failure of the driving machine resulted in insufficiency in air supply and, subsequently, low cardiac output. The remaining 17 calves of the first group died by low cardiac output (4 calves), pulmonary insufficiency (6), thromboembolism (3), and unknown causes (4). Unknown causes were characterized by quick deterioration which occurred within 50 hr. In the second group death was mainly due to pathophysiological causes: thromboembolism (9 calves, 36%), infection (4), breakage of the device (4), low cardiac output (3), renal dysfunction (2), severe pulmonary edema due to unknown causes (1), tear of aorta (1), and accident (1). Clinical symptoms of thromboembolism were mainly related to the brain, lung, and kidney, and histopathologic studies revealed small infarcts, hemorrhages, and necrosis in those organs. Low cardiac output resulted from 2 causes: one from inadequate performance of TAH, and the other from inflow interference in the inferior vena cava. Four deaths due to severe gram negative infection by E. coli and P. aeruginosa were seen in the early stage.
