VL  - 8
IS  - 4
SP  - 211
EP  - 219
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0033380964?origin=resultslist
AB  - Concern exists in Britain about waiting lists for elective treatment and the increased demand for inpatient beds during winter. An area of interest has been patients with multiple readmissions over one or two years. In this study, Birmingham residents with at least four finished consultant episodes in 1996-7 were identified using the contract minimum data set. Care pathways of patients readmitted on three or more occasions were assessed using logistic regression. 65 204 records were identified, representing 28% of all inpatient episodes (n = 234 334). A total of 0.7% of Birmingham's population (6541 out of 960 940) accounted for this large number of episodes, once admissions for haemodialysis and intravenous infusion were excluded. Of these, 4437 subjects were readmitted on three or more occasions. There were three peaks: 2 year old children, adults in their late 20s, and the elderly in their mid-70s. Children and pregnant patients of Asian origin were significantly over represented. Nearly 70% of patients were admitted as an emergency at least once (n=3013) and 10% for non-specific or ill defined disorders. Cancer was associated with increased mortality while respiratory disease, childhood haematological disorders, gastrointestinal disease, stroke, or heart disease were associated with increased length of stay and emergency readmission. Social deprivation and comorbidity were associated with longer stays in non-elderly adults. After adjusting for demography and case mix, the hospital of admission did not generally show an association with mortality, emergency admission, or length of stay. However, lengths of stay for the elderly and readmissions of patients under 65 years old were significantly higher at one site. Addressing the needs of patients with multiple admissions could reduce the demand for hospital beds. The comparison of hospital mortality rates is, however, of little value.
