TI  - Incipient localized skin necroses ('pressure marks' or 'blisters'). Occurrence in a medicolegal autopsy material, particularly in cases of barbiturate poisoning (Danish)
PY  - 1975
T2  - Ugeskrift for Laeger
VL  - 137
IS  - 7
SP  - 378
EP  - 384
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0016438949?origin=resultslist
AB  - In 463 cases of fatal poisoning socalled pressure marks (incipient skin necroses (i.s.n.), blisters), were located. These pressure marks at first appear as red, well defined plaques, occasionally with central cyanosis or pallor. Later they may turn bluish red, the epidermis may be detached and (very seldom in this material) a bulla may develop. The cases are divided into 2 groups: Group I comprising pure barbiturate poisoning (184 cases) and Group II comprising combined poisoning with barbiturate and another drug or other drugs. In Group I the frequency of the pressure marks was generally found to be 23% with significantly higher occurrence in females, and a large number (62%) of the pressure marks developed already during a maxiumum of 24 hr coma. The typical localization was at sites subjected to pressure, but seldom at maximum pressure areas. About 75% of the pressure marks were found on legs and feet. The group with pressure marks did not exhibit higher blood concentrations of barbiturate as compared with the group without. The maximal possible duration of poisoning in each case is used as a measure of the duration of the poisoning, which was found to be 45.6 hr for cases with pressure marks and 73.2 hr for cases with none. In Group II, there was a frequency of pressure marks of 29% significantly higher than that of Group I. In addition, a discription is given of pressure marks in cases poisoned with e.g. alcohol, morphine, amitriptyline, nitrous oxide, methyprylone, diazepam, chloral hydrate, chlorprotixene, metaqualone, salicylic acid and dextropropoxiphene (Group III). The macroscopic appearance and the localization of the pressure marks did not differ from those of the pressure marks in Groups I and II. Finally, the study includes 10 cases of pressure marks in which no ingestion of drug has taken place. Autopsy revealed the following causes of death: Suffocation (3), heart failure, traumatic epilepsy, diabetic coma, lobar pneumonia, cerebral hemorrhage and 2 deaths from unknown causes. Hypotheses advanced concerning the pathogenesis, and particularly the view that pressure is supposed to be the sole cause are disputed. It is stated that pressure marks develop only during coma. Hypoxia, direct toxic action of drugs on the epidermis and slight local pressure are presumed to be the essential factors in connection with the onset of the pressure marks.
