TI  - The DOA autopsy in army hospitals
PY  - 1975
T2  - Military Medicine
VL  - 140
IS  - 12
SP  - 857
EP  - 858
DO  - 10.1093/milmed/140.12.857
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0016776328?origin=resultslist
AB  - Army hospitals traditionally have had high autopsy rates, in part because of the commander's prerogative on active duty personnel, but also because of emphasis on the value of the procedure to dependent and retired persons, on whom permission for autopsy  must be obtained. In 1972, there were 4,532 deaths of which 2,960 led to autopsy (65%). Two thirds of the  deaths were inpatients, while one third were dead on arrival (DOA; including stillbirths). The autopsy rate on inpatients was 68%; on the DOA group, 59%. In 1973 the data were similar. The large group of inpatient autopsies is not at issue here: the postmortem examination remains a major learning method. Clinicopathologic correlations are possible with inpatients, and the autopsy study should be pursued to whatever extent the staff feels necessary. From the overall point of view, as a measure of the quality of patient care, the autopsy is outstanding. The DOA autopsies generally lack the clinicopathologic  correlation, however, and a high autopsy rate in this group of cases may no longer be a suitable command goal. An analysis of the above mentioned 1972 DOA autopsy series shows 40% of the cases were in the accident, poisoning and violence field, 20% involved the cardiovascular system, and 18% were stillbirths; the rest were in other categories, including 9% called ill defined deaths. The ill defined group needed autopsies to determine a cause of death. The reasons behind all of these DOA autopsies are presumed commendable. In a time and  in a place of unlimited pathology resources, these cases can be done. In the current time of austere staffing, however, the value of these routine autopsies seems questionable.
