AB  - An autopsy study was made of cardiac death from diseases of unknown cause with or without hypertrophy, death with organic changes of the heart, cerebral hemorrhage, diseases of the liver, kidney or lung, bleeding, asphyxia, head injuries, poisoning (from hypnotics, carbon monoxide etc.) and other external causes (such as fat embolism, electrocution, and drug shock). The MDH (malate dehydrogenase) content in the blood serum, brain, heart, lung, liver, kidney, spleen and muscle was determined by the Mehler Dumont Kato method. It was found that the MDH values in the serum were higher in cardiac deaths with organic changes of the heart than in other cases. The MDH values in the right and left heart ventricles were lower in cases of cardiac death with organic changes of the heart and in deaths from cerebral hemorrhage than in other cases. The MDH values in the liver were slightly lower in deaths from liver or kidney diseases than in other cases. The MDH content in the serum or in the organs was not related to age or sex.
