AB  - According to the Mortality Statistics of the Danish Health Board, the incidence of sudden unexpected infantile death of unknown cause (SIDS) has increased in Denmark from 0.34 in the period 1972-1975 to 1.2 in the period 1980-1982. A register investigation based upon the Register of Causes of Death of the Danish Health Board in 1972-1982 revealed considerable regional differences in incidence which could be correlated to the three medico-legal institutes concerned and a difference in the basis of diagnosis in the regions served by the three medico-legal institutes. SIDS is a diagnosis by exclusion without positive diagnostic criteria and, according to the definition, the diagnosis can only be established on the basis of autopsy. The basis for diagnosis was found to be significantly different in the three regions served by the medico-legal institutes. This alone, however, cannot explain neither the difference in incidence registered or the regional difference in incidence. Interpretation of autopsy results such as inflammation, aspiration and non-lethal malformations and alteration in diagnostic practice in the course of time may partially explain both the regional difference in incidence and the increase in incidence in the entire country. Even in a little country like Denmark, regional differences in the interpretation of infantile deaths appear to play a considerable role in registration. In assessing materials from abroad, it is important to recognize the considerable difficulties, which exist in delimitation of a syndrome such as SIDS, which is not defined by the occurrence of positive diagnostic criteria but by the absence of these.
