EP  - 266
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0018832210?origin=resultslist
AB  - In order to illustrate the certainty with which the main cause of death is stated in general practice, complete autopsy was undertaken in 45 sudden unexpected but not medicolegal deaths which were brought to Sundby Hospital. Following autopsy, it could be demonstrated that ischaemic heart disease was overdiagnosed prior to autopsy. In the group of patients with ischaemic heart disease, acute myocardial infarction was over-diagnosed. Pulmonary embolism, rupture of the aorta and malignant conditions were underdiagnosed on the death certificates. Even although compensatory equalizations occurred, these could not counteract the effect of the incorrectly stated causes of death. In all, disagreement between the death certificate and the autopsy findings occurred in 27 cases (60%). Restraint is advised in the use of coronary thrombosis as the cause of death unless this has been proved. In obscure cases death should be stated to be due to unknown causes.
