AB  - The intramyocardial substances in human cadavers from several endogenous acute deaths have been investigated in connection with the cause of death, by primary reference to the glycogen level, with the results as follows. 1. The myocardium showed a decline of pH and increased lactate and glycogen concentrations following 'exogenous acute deaths' (from oligemia, asphyxia, brain injury or traumatic shock). 2. Findings in cadavers from 'pathological acute deaths due to heart failure' associated with apparent organic changes were essentially the same as in the foregoing group. 3. The groups of 'cardiac deaths of unknown etiology' and of 'deaths due to drug-induced shock' presented features distinct from the foregoing two groups: there was a rise in myocardial pH with significantly lower lactate and glycogen levels. 4. These are concerned in both groups with the myocardial glycogen level prior to death, which might possibly be profoundly influenced by existing hypoxia or anoxia. Other etiologic factors are yet to be investigated as well in cardiac deaths of unknown cause and in deaths due to drug-induced shock
