TI  - Cerebrovascular accidents in Poland: deaths and medical attendance rates
ST  - UDARY MOZGU W POLSCE: ZGONY I ZGLOSZENIA DO LEKARZA
PY  - 1973
T2  - Neurologia i Neurochirurgia Polska
VL  - 23
IS  - 6
SP  - 777
EP  - 784
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0015683971?origin=resultslist
AB  - The diagnosis of cerebrovascular accidents (CVA) on death certificates and in materials obtained during a survey on medical attendance in Poland in the yr 1967 to 1968 have been analyzed. CVA death rates in 1970 were: 37.8 per 100,000 in the total population and above 700 in the age group above 65 yr. Death rates were somewhat lower for females and rural population. CVA appeared to be the 4th cause of deaths in Poland. It is pointed out, however, that these rates are underestimated and probable reasons for this underestimation are discussed. The incidence rate of CVA based upon the results of a medical attendance survey in Poland was 123 per 100,000, the prevalence of the disease was 195 per 100,000 of the total population, and 1,200 per 100,000 in the age group of 65 yr and above. Significant differences in death and attendance rates were found between the large cities and between the provinces in Poland. In the group of cities the rates of mortality and attendance were highest in Warsaw and lowest in Cracow. The diagnosis of cerebral hemorrhage was found in 63% of death certificates and in only 23% of those who attended health service institutions because of this disease. The percentage of other and ill defined types of strokes were 19% and 63% respectively.
