TY  - JOUR
AU  - Medeiros, A.
TI  - Hunger kills two infants a day in Baixada
ST  - Fome mata 2 criancas por dia na Baixada.
PY  - 1991
T2  - Planejamento Agora
VL  - 7
IS  - 213
SP  - 1
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0026422928?origin=resultslist
AB  - The conclusions of the detailed reporting of the journal O Dia on deaths of children in 6 regional municipalities of Brazil are presented. The findings on children up to age 2 are more complete than official statistics. For instance, the most recent data from the State Secretary of Health referred to the first 4 months of 1989 based on reported cases. In contrast, the newspapers researched the obituary records of 17 record-offices of Baixada Fluminense, verified the registries of the major children's hospital of the region, collected data from the Sao Salvador funerary home, checked the record books of the main cemeteries where many children who were victims of starvation were buried. The results showed that every 10 hours a child dies of hunger in Baixada. In the 17 record-offices and in the obituary records themselves hunger (diagnosed by doctors as malnutrition) did not appear in explicit form; in the month of May, 1991, the books registered only 12 cases of child deaths caused by malnutrition. In the majority of cases, they were entered with the notation: cause undetermined. In May 1968, death certificates of children were issued with undetermined cause of death. In almost 100% of these cases, the children died of malnutrition at home, and even calling a doctor would not have provided a chance of survival. The doctors register the cause as undetermined, as they are unable to diagnose the cause. This artifice has been official since a resolution of the State Secretary of Health was adopted in February, 1990. Fewer than 90% of undetermined causes of death are attributable to malnutrition, according to the director of the children's hospital of Nova Iguacu. The precise registration of the death of children is not much in currency among poor families in Baixada, although the parents often carry out the registration accurately. On the other hand, the director of the community council of Nova Iguacu stated that although the assessment by 0 Dia was more complete and up-to-date than official data, it still did not completely reflect the real situation. There were children who did not get into any kind of statistics and do not form part of any registry; those dying at home who get buried at the farm. These are invisible deaths, as these children are rarely registered when they are born.
