TY  - JOUR
TI  - Suicide statistics: the problem of comparability
PY  - 1975
T2  - World Health Organization Chronicle
VL  - 29
IS  - 5
SP  - 188
EP  - 193
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0016799842?origin=resultslist
AB  - A questionnaire on procedures for the ascertainment of suicide was drawn up and sent to interested persons in 24 countries. The inescapable conclusion is that the present official suicide statistics are of only limited value. Constructing epidemiological or sociodemographic theories about suicide, therefore, will remain hazardous until more effort is spent studying how suicide is ascertained and until the statistics can be made more reliable. To this end the following steps might be taken. A standard definition of suicide might be promulgated, comparable to that for stillbirth which has been used to improve the comparability of perinatal mortality. A space might be provided on the death certificate for uniformly recording whether a violent death was due to suicide, accident, homicide, or undetermined causes, and, in the last instance, whether suicide was thought probable; the effects on the statistics could then be studied. A study of case material from countries where the decision on suicide is made by either medically qualified or legally qualified persons might show how widely applicable is the finding that doctors report suicide more readily than lawyers.
