AB  - In 104 autopsies a study was made on the cause of death as selected to the postmortem lactic acid content and the pH of the myocardium. In Group I of deaths due to exogenous causes the lactic acid content was higher (death due to poisoning caused by carbon monoxide and freezing death)<Group II (death by fire and death by drowning)ΩGroup III (death due to damage to the cervical region of the spinal cord, death due to damage to the brain, and death due to suffocation caused by endotracheal obstruction)<Group IV (death due to hemorrhage and loss of blood and death due to damage to the internal organs); the pH of the head muscle showed a reversed sequence accordingly. In deaths due to diseases, there were 2 groups. In patients who had died of peritonitis, pneumonia, various cancers and liver diseases, the lactic acid content and pH remained similar to Groups II and III of deaths due to exogenous causes. In patients who had died from myocardiopathy cadavers and patients with acute heart failure gave similar findings to Group III, but those who died of so called cardiac failure due to an unknown cause or of shock gave findings rather similar to Group I, with the lactic acid content being very low and pH being high. Therefore, it appears that the postmortem lactic acid content and pH in the heart muscle in death due to exogenous causes reflect the antemortem anoxia and are associated with the duration of the disease, while in other cases of death, and in death due to diseases, the proportion of these substances varies with the pathologic nature of the disease.
