TI  - The autopsy: its role in modern medicine
PY  - 1976
T2  - Hawaii Medical Journal
VL  - 35
IS  - 1
SP  - 14
EP  - 16
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0017264061?origin=resultslist
AB  - The autopsy from its inception to the present day has played an important role in medical education and research. In the USA, it was utilized effectively to assess and maintain the quality of medical care from 1928 to 1973. The elimination of a minimal autopsy rate for hospital accreditation by the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Hospitals (JCAH) in 1973 has resulted in further eclipsing of the role of the autopsy in modern medicine. This and other factors are responsible for a decline in an era when the autopsy should be of even greater value in the quality assessment of medical care, namely, with the advent of medical auditing. Why has there been a decline in the autopsy rate? From the clinician's perspective there has been a definite decrease in interest in autopsies as a result of: better diagnostic techniques, hence fewer deaths from unknown causes; less emphasis in medical school and specialty training on anatomical pathology in general, with more emphasis on pathophysiology and molecular biology; poor communication between pathologists and clinicians; a fear of liability; poor, dull protocols with too often the cause of death being undiscovered, and the observation that pathologists sometimes perform autopsies begrudgingly. Still the autopsy is of increasing importance in the quality assessment of medical care, that is, the auditing of medical care. The autopsy has been and still is one of the major modalities for the detection of errors in diagnosis and therapy. Some of these errors are of purely academic interest, while others are matters of life and death. Bauer and Robbin's (1972) review of 2,734 autopsies indicated that there were serious clinical errors in 40% of them. All autopsies that reveal mistakes, whether purely academic or serious, are of importance in sharpening clinical acumen. However, compelled by the economic realities of the existing situation the author suggests certain priorities and decision making criteria: Where there are physical limitations as to time, space, and personnel, services for living patients take precedence over those for dead ones. The value of an autopsy, and therefore the decision to perform it, can only be measured in terms of its contribution to the clarification of a diagnosis, the clarification of complications of management, the training of medical and paramedical personnel, and as a contribution to medical progress and knowledge. Medical insurance should cover the cost of autopsies (about $400) and this coverage should be part of an overall national health insurance plan.
